-- what a control keeps for itself --
  every kind this platform builds was asked: true
  and every one of them answered: true
  no control keeps a negative amount: true
  exactly the containers that draw chrome keep any: true
  and only a disclosure can be opened: true
  a group box keeps a border, and more of it where the title is: true
-- a disclosure --
  its header is chrome at the top and nothing else: true
  it holds children like any other box: true
  a user opens it loudly and a program quietly: true
-- a tab view --
  its children are its pages, and its labels are not: true
  a page that moves takes its label with it: true
  a page sits where the tab view puts it, not where it is told: true
  a page that is not there is refused, and so is none at all: true
  a user choosing a tab raises the page it chose: true
-- a split view --
  its handle keeps room on the axis it sits on: true
  a third pane is refused, and so is a handle off the control: true
  a program moves the handle quietly, and a resize is not a drag: true
  a user dragging the handle raises where it landed: true
-- a layout node from a control that is gone --
  a live container still becomes one: true
  a released one is refused, not taken for keeping nothing: true
  a teardown the platform refuses says so: true
  and finishes anyway: true
-- a scroll view --
  the viewport is the height it was given: true
  what it scrolls over is taller: true
  a dragged divider resizes the controls inside its pane: true
  a released split view is not arranged on a guess: true
drove=true
